# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Lecture 46 vignette pool C -- Cytopenias and Hemochromatosis (Ward), slides 61-107: hemolysis labs and workup,
autoimmune hemolytic anemia, glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, thalassemia, sickle cell disease,
hemochromatosis, and the lifespan / follow-up tables.

KEYS ARE WRITTEN SHORT ON PURPOSE -- detail lives in the explanation.
EXPLANATIONS follow tools/question_writing/EXPLAIN_DEEP.md: the key names the decisive clue and the slide fact;
each wrong choice says why it fails for THIS patient, the replacing fact, and a "Tell:".

Normal ranges in stems: total bilirubin 0.3-1.2 mg/dL is the slide's (s67). The deck gives no range for hemoglobin,
reticulocytes, haptoglobin, lactate dehydrogenase, ferritin or transferrin saturation, so stems carry conventional
adult reference ranges (hemoglobin 12.0-16.0 g/dL women / 13.5-17.5 g/dL men, reticulocytes 0.5-2.5%, haptoglobin
30-200 mg/dL, lactate dehydrogenase 140-280 U/L, ferritin 12-300 micrograms/L, transferrin saturation 20-50%). The
iron-overload items never put a value between 45% and 50% saturation, so the slide's <300 / <45% rule (s103) and
the printed range never disagree on a stem's value.

Deliberately NOT keyed: chloroquine (scope ban; antimalarials only as a class), s83, beta thalassemia ancestry, the
week of chorionic villus sampling, the location of the enzyme (s77 vs s79), why the enzyme level is not drawn during
an attack (s82 asks but does not answer). The glucose-6-phosphate items name a single s79 trigger as the key and use
non-trigger choices (slide treatments such as hydration and transfusion, or drugs absent from the s79 list) as
distractors; no distractor is claimed "safe" beyond "not on the list of common triggers".
"""
